UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 26, Lesson 2: Safe crossing role plays', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Role plays let us practise safe crossing without any danger. Use chalk or tape for a pretend road and crossing, and play pedestrian, driver or adult. Say the steps aloud: stop, look, listen, think, walk. Practising steps again and again builds safe habits. Role plays happen away from real roads, and everyone follows the rules.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same idea to a new game or situation, then compare two choices for safety and inclusion.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nIn a role play, the pedestrian steps off the kerb while looking at a phone. What is the problem and what should they do instead?\n\nFEEDBACK\nLooking at a phone means they cannot see or hear vehicles. They should put the phone away, stop at the kerb and use stop, look, listen, think. Pedestrians need all their senses.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking you only need to look in one direction. Vehicles can come from both directions, so look right, left and right again.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nSet up a pretend road with chalk or masking tape and role play three crossing situations (a zebra crossing, traffic lights and a driveway). Use cards with each step. Record what the pedestrian and the driver need to do in each, and swap roles.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one choice you changed and why. Any movement is optional, adult-supervised and can be done in a gentler way or described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"PDHPE","learning_intention":"Role plays let us practise safe crossing without any danger. Use chalk or tape for a pretend road and crossing, and play pedestrian, driver or adult. Say the steps aloud: stop, look, listen, think, walk. Practising steps again and again builds safe habits. Role plays happen away from real roads, and everyone follows the rules.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Practise and apply","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Clear, safe movement space with an adult nearby for any physical activity","Optional: soft ball, cushion or water bottle"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["PH2-IHW-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Set up a pretend road with chalk or masking tape and role play three crossing situations (a zebra crossing, traffic lights and a driveway). Use cards with each step. Record what the pedestrian and the driver need to do i Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Looking at a phone means they cannot see or hear vehicles. They should put the phone away, stop at the kerb and use stop, look, listen, think. Pedestrians need all their senses. Reasoning: I used a role play by attending to a chalk crossing rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Thinking you only need to look in one direction. Vehicles can come from both directions, so look right, left and right again. Correction: Looking at a phone means they cannot see or hear vehicles. They should put the phone away, stop at the kerb and use stop, look, listen, think. Pedestrians… Why: the correction restores accurate use of a role play.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking you only need to look in one direction. Vehicles can come from both directions, so look right, left and right again. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating a role play on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with a role play, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Looking at a phone means they cannot see or hear vehicles. They should put the phone away, stop at the kerb and use stop, look, listen, think. Pedestrians… This matches the lesson skill for a role play.","options":["Looking at a phone means they cannot see or hear vehicles. They should put the phone away, stop at the kerb and use stop, look, listen, think. Pedestrians…","Thinking you only need to look in one direction. Vehicles can come from both directions, so look right, left and right again.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting.","Copy the example and call that independent work."],"question":"In a role play, the pedestrian steps off the kerb while looking at a phone. What is the problem and what should they do instead?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Thinking you only need to look in one direction. Vehicles can come from both directions, so look right, left and right again. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Careful use of a chalk crossing with clear evidence.","Thinking you only need to look in one direction. Vehicles can come from both directions, so look right, left and right again.","Role plays let us practise safe crossing without any danger. Use chalk or tape for a pretend road and crossing, and play pedestrian, driver or adult. Say the…","Looking at a phone means they cannot see or hear vehicles. They should put the phone away, stop at the kerb and use stop, look, listen, think. Pedestrians…"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising a role play?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named a chalk crossing, not by copying the model.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a skipped permission or safety step as if it did not matter.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the a chalk crossing used."],"question":"A learner is practising a role play. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Looking at a phone means they cannot see or hear vehicles. They should put the phone away, stop at the kerb and use stop, look, listen, think. Pedestrians… uses safe, healthy, respectful choices appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting.","Copy the example and call that independent work.","Looking at a phone means they cannot see or hear vehicles. They should put the phone away, stop at the kerb and use stop, look, listen, think. Pedestrians…","Thinking you only need to look in one direction. Vehicles can come from both directions, so look right, left and right again."],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about a role play?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","Thinking you only need to look in one direction. Vehicles can come from both directions, so look right, left and right again.","Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about a role play.","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about a chalk crossing?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Example: on a chalk crossing in the backyard, Dev is the pedestrian, Anna is the driver with a cardboard car and Mum is the adult. Dev…","Thinking you only need to look in one direction. Vehicles can come from both directions, so look right, left and right again.","Treat a skipped permission or safety step as if it did not matter.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Role plays let us practise safe crossing without any danger. Use chalk or tape for a pretend road and… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every online claim as equally reliable.","Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Thinking you only need to look in one direction. Vehicles can come from both directions, so look right, left and right again.","Set up a pretend road with chalk or masking tape and role play three crossing situations (a zebra crossing, traffic lights and a driveway). Use…","Retype the worked example with no new example.","Copy the example and call that independent work."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of a role play?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating a role play on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Looking at a phone means they cannot see or hear vehicles. They should put the phone away, stop at the kerb and use stop, look, listen, think. Pedestrians need all their senses. Reasoning: I used a role play by attending to a chalk crossing rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with a role play, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Thinking you only need to look in one direction. Vehicles can come from both directions, so look right, left and right again. Correction: Looking at a phone means they cannot see or hear vehicles. They should put the phone away, stop at the kerb and use stop, look, listen, think. Pedestrians… Why: the correction restores accurate use of a role play.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-pdhpe-y3-w26-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA PDHPE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (PH2-*); content-point mapping remains pending human review. Physical activities are optional, adult-supervised and adaptable; no child is required to share personal details.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise a role play.","Read the mission before you start moving or deciding.","Keep your own safety and everyone''s inclusion in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not keep everyone safe and included."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the cue or rule that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore safety, consent or inclusion.","Prefer a reasoned, respectful choice over one that merely looks fun."],"submit":["Confirm that your reasons and reflection are both present.","Proofread names, steps and safety words.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact cue, rule or choice before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key cues.","Say what each cue does for safety, health or friendship.","A final label alone is weaker than a stated reason."],"teach2":["Compare an unsafe or unkind choice with a better one.","Change the choice or strategy, not only the words.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, game or situation that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original reasons and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill in a new situation."]},"subject":"Healthy, Safe and Active Lifestyles","success_criteria":["I can use a role play with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify a chalk crossing and explain why it matters.","I can practise pedestrian and driver independently on a new prompt.","I can notice swapping roles needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Role plays let us practise safe crossing without any danger. Use chalk or tape for a pretend road and crossing, and play pedestrian, driver or adult. Say the steps aloud: stop, look, listen, think, walk. Practising steps again and again builds safe habits. Role plays happen…","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason.","Role plays let us practise safe crossing without any danger. Use chalk or tape for a pretend road and crossing, and play pedestrian, driver or adult. Say the steps aloud: stop, look, listen, think, walk. Practising steps again and again builds safe habits. Role plays happen away from real roads, and everyone follows the rules.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a role play?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: on a chalk crossing in the backyard, Dev is the pedestrian, Anna is the driver with a cardboard car and Mum is the adult. Dev says aloud, ''Stop. Look right, left, right. Listen. Think: the car has stopped, so I will walk.'' They swap roles so Dev sees what a driver can see.\n\nTRY FIRST\nIn a role play, the pedestrian steps off the kerb while looking at a phone. What is the problem and what should they do instead?\n\nFEEDBACK\nLooking at a phone means they cannot see or hear vehicles. They should put the phone away, stop at the kerb and use stop, look, listen, think. Pedestrians need all their senses.","explain":"Role plays let us practise safe crossing without any danger. Use chalk or tape for a pretend road and crossing, and play pedestrian, driver or adult. Say the steps aloud: stop, look, listen, think, walk. Practising steps again and again builds safe habits. Role plays happen away from real roads, and everyone follows the rules.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking you only need to look in one direction. Vehicles can come from both directions, so look right, left and right again. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"A chalk crossing role play.","caption":"Practise on chalk roads.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Pedestrian</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">says each step aloud</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Driver</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">cardboard car stops</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: on a chalk crossing in the backyard, Dev is the pedestrian, Anna is the driver with a cardboard car and Mum is  Then write your own reasons.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.\nJuno: Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: on a chalk crossing in the backyard, Dev is the pedestrian, Anna is the driver with a cardboard car and Mum is  Then write your own reasons."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason.","Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a role play?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: on a chalk crossing in the backyard, Dev is the pedestrian, Anna is the driver with a cardboard car and Mum is the adult. Dev says aloud, ''Stop. Look right, left, right. Listen. Think: the car has stopped, so I will walk.'' They swap roles so Dev sees what a driver can see. Look at each cue, rule or choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key cues, rules or choices from the example and say what each one does for safety, health or relationships.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\".","explain":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Show the reasoning","visual":[{"alt":"Phone and senses at a crossing.","caption":"Use every sense.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Phone</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">eyes down, ears busy</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Safe</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">phone away, senses ready</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a role play?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking you only need to look in one direction. Vehicles can come from both directions, so look right, left and right again. Here is a more helpful example: Example: on a chalk crossing in the backyard, Dev is the pedestrian, Anna is the driver with a cardboard car and Mum is the adult. Dev says aloud, ''Stop. Look right, left, right. Listen. Think: the car has stopped, so I will walk.'' They swap roles so Dev sees what a driver can see.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice does not work, then write a safer, kinder or healthier choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nLooking at a phone means they cannot see or hear vehicles. They should put the phone away, stop at the kerb and use stop, look, listen, think. Pedestrians need all their senses.","explain":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Thinking you only need to look in one direction. Vehicles can come from both directions, so look right, left and right again. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason.","Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a role play?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nRole plays let us practise safe crossing without any danger. Use chalk or tape for a pretend road and crossing, and play pedestrian, driver or adult. Say the steps aloud: stop, look, listen, think, walk. Practising steps again and again builds safe habits. Role plays happen away from real roads, and everyone follows the rules. Now choose a different situation to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe or practise one fresh situation that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people.","explain":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Transfer to a new situation"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 26, Lesson 2: Safe crossing role plays","unit_number":6,"unit_title":"Staying safe","week":26,"week_in_unit":1,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: on a chalk crossing in the backyard, Dev is the pedestrian, Anna is the driver with a cardboard car and Mum is the adult. Dev says aloud, ''Stop. Look right, left, right. Listen. Think: the car has stopped, so I will walk.'' They swap roles so Dev sees what a driver can see.\n\nWrite what makes this example safe, healthy or respectful, naming the cues, rules or choices used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"A chalk crossing role play.","caption":"Practise on chalk roads.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Pedestrian</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">says each step aloud</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Driver</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">cardboard car stops</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Phone and senses at a crossing.","caption":"Use every sense.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Phone</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">eyes down, ears busy</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Safe</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">phone away, senses ready</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-pdhpe-y3-w26-l2' AND family_id IS NULL;
